## Phase 3 — Route Coverage (post-level-10 obstacles)

**Goal:** After level 10 each round, players cannot "shortcut" — they must paint the intended path or hit dead ends.

### P3.1 Option A — One-way gates (recommended first)
- New cell type: `gate` — passable only if trail already covers an adjacent "key" cell.
- Place gate on shortest-path shortcuts during generation for `levelNumber > 10`.
- BFS solvability must include gate state: `(position, trailMask)` or precompute required cells.

### P3.2 Option B — Mandatory trail coverage
- Mark `requiredCells: TPosition[]` during generation (cells on the unique/minimum path corridor).
- Win condition: `didWin && requiredCells.every(p => trail[p.row][p.col])`.
- Show required cells as faint gold overlay until painted.

### P3.3 Option C — Ice stops (activate existing type)
- **Ice = stoppable surface** (slide stops on ice even without wall).
- Levels 11+: increase ice density along corridors; shortcuts lack ice → overshoot into dead end.
- Update `slide()` and `minMovesToGoal` BFS to match.

### P3.4 Level generator changes (`generateLevel`)
For `levelNumber > 10`:
1. Generate base maze (current logic).
2. Compute shortest path cells via BFS parent map.
3. Identify shortcut cells (high betweenness or off main path).
4. Place walls/gates/ice to block shortcuts while keeping solvable.
5. Reject layouts where multiple trivial paths exist (optional).

### P3.5 Loophole prevention checklist
- [ ] BFS uses same `slide()` semantics as gameplay (including ice/gates).
- [ ] Goal remains a stoppable cell.
- [ ] No soft-lock: player can always Reset.
- [ ] Unit tests per mechanic in `__tests__/gameLogic.test.ts`.

